            "description": "Watch Sturm Graz vs Heart Of Midlothian highlights and goals from UEFA Champions League on ReFooty: Football Highlights Hub. Match played on July 21, 2026. On July 21, 2026, the European stage was set for a compelling UEFA Champions League encounter as Sturm Graz hosted Heart Of Midlothian . Both clubs stepped onto the pitch with the weight of expectation on their shoulders, eager to secure a vital result in this prestigious competition. Early Dominance from the Hosts The match ignited almost immediately as Sturm Graz took control from the opening whistle. In just the 4th minute, the home fans were sent into raptures when Jon Gorenc-Stankovic found the back of the net, clinical in his finish following an assist from Jeyland Mitchell . This early breakthrough forced Heart Of Midlothian onto the back foot, struggling to maintain possession against a high-pressing Austrian side. The remainder of the first half was a tactical battle, with the visitors tightening their defense to prevent further damage before the interval. A Second-Half Goal Blitz The momentum shifted gear decisively after the restart. Following a tactical substitution that saw Otar Kiteishvili enter the fray, Sturm Graz unleashed a devastating attacking sequence. In the 50th minute, Jeyland Mitchell turned from provider to scorer, slotting home thanks to a Kiteishvili assist. The floodgates were well and truly open; just three minutes later, Luca Weinhandl added a third after being set up by Seedy Jatta . The scoring was rounded off in the 59th minute by Juergen Heil , completing a remarkable period where the hosts effectively secured the points in less than ten minutes. Clinical Finish and Defensive Solidity Despite a flurry of substitutions from Heart Of Midlothian manager in an attempt to spark a comeback, the Scottish side found no way through a disciplined Sturm Graz backline. Yellow cards for Christian Dahle Borchgrevink , Jon Gorenc-Stankovic , and Claudio Braga late in the game highlighted the competitive edge of the contest. As the final whistle blew after five minutes of stoppage time, Sturm Graz celebrated a professional and dominant performance that left their opponents with plenty of questions to answer."
